  "content": {
    "id": "p21-q9-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "Prove that $\\sqrt{1+\\sqrt{2}}$ is irrational.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\sqrt{1+\\sqrt{2}}$ is irrational.\n\nIt is a root of $P(x)=x^4-2x^2-1$, whose only possible rational roots are $\\pm1$; neither is a root.",
      "worked": "Let $a=\\sqrt{1+\\sqrt{2}}$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}a^2&=1+\\sqrt{2}\\\\a^2-1&=\\sqrt{2}\\\\(a^2-1)^2&=2\\\\a^4-2a^2+1&=2\\\\a^4-2a^2-1&=0.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThus $a$ is a root of the monic polynomial $P(x)=x^4-2x^2-1$ with integer coefficients. By the Integer Root Theorem, its only possible rational roots are $1$ and $-1$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}P(1)&=1^4-2(1)^2-1=-2\\\\P(-1)&=(-1)^4-2(-1)^2-1=-2.\\end{align*}$$\n\nNeither candidate is a root, so $P(x)$ has no rational roots. Therefore $\\sqrt{1+\\sqrt{2}}$ is irrational.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 85
  },
